Both compile, both export a symbol named [38;5;10mdriver[0m, and [38;5;10mnm -D[0m parity was clean — but the ABIs differ. The C reads its argument from the vector register ([38;5;10mmovss %xmm0,-0x14(%rbp)[0m); the Rust version read the integer register ([38;5;10mmov %edi,%ebx[0m). Any caller passing a float got unrelated bytes back: for [38;5;10mdriver(1.0f)[0m C printed [38;5;10m0000803f[0m while Rust printed whatever was left in [38;5;10m%edi[0m. After the fix the export begins [38;5;10mmovd %xmm0,%ebx[0m and matches C for every input.[0m[0m
